  "content": {
    "id": "p41-q25-root",
    "type": "question",
    "label": "25",
    "prompt": "For $x=\\sqrt{2}+1$ and $y=\\sqrt{5}-2$, find in simplest form.",
    "layout": "grid",
    "columns": 2,
    "questionDiagrams": [],
    "children": [
      {
        "id": "p41-q25-a",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "a",
        "prompt": "$\\dfrac{1}{x}$",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "$\\sqrt{2}-1$",
          "worked": "$$\\begin{align*}\\frac{1}{x}&=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}+1}\\\\\\frac{1}{x}&=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}+1}\\times\\frac{\\sqrt{2}-1}{\\sqrt{2}-1}\\\\\\frac{1}{x}&=\\frac{\\sqrt{2}-1}{2-1}\\\\\\frac{1}{x}&=\\sqrt{2}-1\\end{align*}$$",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 48
      },
      {
        "id": "p41-q25-b",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "b",
        "prompt": "$\\dfrac{1}{x}+\\dfrac{1}{y}$",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "$\\sqrt{2}+\\sqrt{5}+1$",
          "worked": "Rationalising the second reciprocal gives\n$$\\begin{align*}\\frac{1}{y}&=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}-2}\\times\\frac{\\sqrt{5}+2}{\\sqrt{5}+2}\\\\\\frac{1}{y}&=\\frac{\\sqrt{5}+2}{5-4}\\\\\\frac{1}{y}&=\\sqrt{5}+2\\end{align*}$$\nUsing part a,\n$$\\begin{align*}\\frac{1}{x}+\\frac{1}{y}&=(\\sqrt{2}-1)+(\\sqrt{5}+2)\\\\\\frac{1}{x}+\\frac{1}{y}&=\\sqrt{2}+\\sqrt{5}+1\\end{align*}$$",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 72
      },
      {
        "id": "p41-q25-c",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "c",
        "prompt": "$\\dfrac{1}{x^2}+\\dfrac{1}{y^2}$",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "$12+4\\sqrt{5}-2\\sqrt{2}$",
          "worked": "Using the rationalised reciprocals from parts a and b,\n$$\\begin{align*}\\frac{1}{x^2}+\\frac{1}{y^2}&=(\\sqrt{2}-1)^2+(\\sqrt{5}+2)^2\\\\\\frac{1}{x^2}+\\frac{1}{y^2}&=(2-2\\sqrt{2}+1)+(5+4\\sqrt{5}+4)\\\\\\frac{1}{x^2}+\\frac{1}{y^2}&=12+4\\sqrt{5}-2\\sqrt{2}\\end{align*}$$",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 48
      },
      {
        "id": "p41-q25-d",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "d",
        "prompt": "$\\dfrac{1}{x}-\\dfrac{1}{y}$",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "$\\sqrt{2}-\\sqrt{5}-3$",
          "worked": "Using the rationalised reciprocals from parts a and b,\n$$\\begin{align*}\\frac{1}{x}-\\frac{1}{y}&=(\\sqrt{2}-1)-(\\sqrt{5}+2)\\\\\\frac{1}{x}-\\frac{1}{y}&=\\sqrt{2}-1-\\sqrt{5}-2\\\\\\frac{1}{x}-\\frac{1}{y}&=\\sqrt{2}-\\sqrt{5}-3\\end{align*}$$",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 46
      }
    ]
